---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Linh Nguyen">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto pt-8 pb-16">
    <a href="/journals" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline"
      >&larr; Back to Journals</a
    >
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mt-8 mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      The Case Against Travel
    </h1>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 mb-8 italic">March 13, 2024</p>
    <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-4">
      <p class="text-lg">
        Read this provocative piece today: <a
          href="https://www.newyorker.com/culture/the-weekend-essay/the-case-against-travel"
          >https://www.newyorker.com/culture/the-weekend-essay/the-case-against-travel</a
        >
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg">
        The headline initially stood out to me because I aspirationally want to
        travel less. Friends would claim this is pretty rich coming from me,
        given that I have a propensity for getting baited into last minute trips
        and expect to be out of town 130+ days this year. But lately I've been
        thinking that there's a balance I should strike with travel. I
        definitely don't want to fall into a stationary, homebody lifestyle
        quite yet… but when you're constantly out of town, many of your
        friendships and relationships are put on pause. I think the cost of this
        is not terribly high when you're a young person in a bustling city.
        (Everyone knows a lot of people and the amount of time allocated to each
        friendship is lower on average. Therefore, traveling more doesn't
        necessarily diminish your relative presence in your friends' lives by as
        much, compared to if you lived in South Bay or an equivalent social
        scene.) Yet, right now most friend hangouts feel like a constant stream
        of "catch-ups." Would be nice to instead build on existing foundations
        and allocate ample time to enjoying each other's company - but I'm just
        gone so often these days.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg">
        Also, I feel so much healthier whenever I stay put in SF. My RHR is a
        shocking 20% lower at home.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg">
        Anyway, turns out the article wasn't related to any of these points
        whatsoever. Some representative quotes:
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        “Travel turns us into the worst version of ourselves while convincing us
        that we're at our best.”
      </blockquote>

      <blockquote>
        “Although people like to talk about their travels, few of us like to
        listen to them. Such talk resembles academic writing and reports of
        dreams: forms of communication driven more by the needs of the producer
        than the consumer.”
      </blockquote>

      <blockquote>
        “It would be one thing to have such a deep passion for falconry that one
        is willing to fly to Abu Dhabi to pursue it, and it would be another
        thing to approach the visit in an aspirational spirit, with the hope of
        developing my life in a new direction. I was in neither position...If
        you are going to see something you neither value nor aspire to value,
        you are not doing much of anything besides locomoting.”
      </blockquote>

      <blockquote>
        ”The tourist is a deferential character. He outsources the vindication
        of his experiences to the ethnologist, to postcards, to conventional
        wisdom about what you are or are not supposed to do in a place. This
        deference, this "openness to experience," is exactly what renders the
        tourist incapable of experience.”
      </blockquote>

      <blockquote>
        ”During my Paris wanderings, I would stare at people, intently
        inspecting their clothing, their demeanor, their interactions. I was
        trying to see the Frenchness in the French people around me. This is not
        a way to make friends.”
      </blockquote>

      <blockquote>
        ”You don't like to think about the fact that someday you will do nothing
        and be nobody. You will only allow yourself to preview this experience
        when you can disguise it in a narrative about how you are doing many
        exciting and edifying things: you are experiencing, you are connecting,
        you are being transformed, and you have the trinkets and photos to prove
        it.”
      </blockquote>

      <p class="text-lg">
        The author has some good points. Most one- or two-week stays in a new
        place tends to be superficial at best. But some of it is calculated
        hyperbole for the sake of being contrarian, I'd imagine.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg">
        One of my core beliefs is that activities that break you out of your
        routine are generally worth pursuing. I didn't love the <a
          href="https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/159615.Transitions"
          ><i>Transitions</i></a
        > book, but did agree with its stance that "separations of space" create
        room for new ideas, possibilities, and ways of being, and help facilitate
        psychological transition during significant junctures in life. Unfamiliar
        circumstances force us to think new thoughts and try new things. Most people
        need the occasional travel to do this. The few true thought leaders out there
        (e.g., Ralph Waldo Emerson, referenced in the article) perhaps do not.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        The author argues that most travel is too fleeting to inspire any deep
        or lasting change. Perhaps true - I would estimate that ~10% of trips
        I've taken have changed me in some meaningful way - whether by
        influencing how I think about a topic, inspiring a new habit or way of
        doing things, or distancing me from my normal surroundings long enough
        to examine a life circumstance with more clarity. Of course, the
        probability of personal change and immersion increases with length of
        stay: three-month trips have had a much more lasting impact than
        one-week trips.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        But my ultimate opinion is this: as long as we're honest that, in the
        other 90% of cases, travel is just for fun, then there's no delusion.
        Next time I travel, I'll make an effort to think more consciously about
        why I choose to do what I do. I'll try to gravitate more towards the
        things I know (music? nature? sports?) so I can more authentically
        appreciate my experiences and explore with more intention.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
